Title:
A Stealthier Reflective Loading

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post analyzes how .NET reflective loading (“execute-assembly”) is detected via ETW, specifically the `Microsoft-Windows-DotNETRuntime` provider emitting `Loader/AssemblyLoad` events.  
- It shows how defenders can capture these events with `logman` and inspect them with PerfView, and notes that assembly metadata like `FullyQualifiedAssemblyName` can be spoofed but may trigger GAC-backed resolution behavior.  
- The core contribution is two “subtler” ETW bypasses that avoid inline/IAT patching of `EtwEventWrite` (and thus avoid suspicious APIs and memory-protection flips): Provider Handle Patching and Subscriber Bit Patching by modifying R/W globals in `clr.dll`’s `.data` section.  
- It also covers an AMSI bypass specific to `clr.dll` by locating and overwriting global variables used by `clr!AmsiScan` (e.g., `amsiScanBuffer`, `g_amsiContext`) to redirect scanning to a fake function.  
- The techniques are implemented in an x64 Cobalt Strike BOF (“inlineExecute”) with flags to disable ETW and/or AMSI and a scan mode to locate patch points across CLR versions.  
- Useful for red teams and offensive tool developers building stealthier in-memory .NET execution; also useful for blue teams to understand where telemetry can be suppressed and what to monitor for tampering.

Technical Focus:
- .NET reflective loading / execute-assembly telemetry
- ETW provider internals in `clr.dll` (DotNETRuntime provider)
- ETW bypass via Provider Handle patching (global handle null/1)
- ETW bypass via Subscriber EnableBits patching (provider enable bitmasks)
- AMSI bypass in `clr.dll` via global pointer/context manipulation
- Signature/pattern scanning of in-memory `clr.dll` code/data across versions

Use Cases:
- Disable/evade `Microsoft-Windows-DotNETRuntime` ETW events during in-memory .NET tool execution
- Build stealthier execute-assembly/BOF loaders without `VirtualProtect`/`WriteProcessMemory`-style patching
- Patch AMSI scanning for assemblies loaded through CLR (non-PowerShell-hosted)
- Develop detections for CLR `.data` tampering of ETW handles/enable bits and AMSI globals
- Red-team tradecraft research on CLR/GAC behavior when spoofing assembly identity

Keywords:
Reflective loading, execute-assembly, ETW, Event Tracing for Windows, Microsoft-Windows-DotNETRuntime, clr.dll, AssemblyLoad, PerfView, logman, EtwEventWrite, CoTemplate, provider handle, EnableBits, .data section, signature scanning, AMSI, AmsiScanBuffer, Cobalt Strike BOF, inlineExecute-Assembly, GAC